description:
email.rs:208-228 — email_copy

The docstring (line 203-207) reads:

  /// Copy Emails from another account (RFC 8621 §4.7 — Email/copy).
  ///
  /// `params` carries `fromAccountId` and optional destroy-after-copy flags.
  /// `create` is a map of creation keys to partial Email objects (with new
  /// mailboxIds etc.) as described in RFC 8621 §4.7.

What's missing: this is the ONLY mail method that crosses accounts. RFC 8620 §5.4 (Foo/copy) defines a method-specific error space that other /set methods do not have:

  - "fromAccountNotFound" — the fromAccountId in params is not an account the user can access
  - "fromAccountNotSupportedByMethod" — the source account does not support Email
  - "stateMismatch" via destroyFromIfInState — the source account state changed since
  - "tooManyCopies" — server-defined cap on copies-per-request
  - "tooManyEmails" — server-defined cap on emails per copy

Plus the standard SetError variants on per-entry create failures: anchorNotFound (when a #-reference is used), invalidProperties, etc.

A caller writing error-handling code against email_copy needs to know:

  1. The cross-account error variants are method-level, not per-entry.
  2. They surface through extract_response as ClientError::MethodError { code, .. }.
  3. The valid codes for email_copy are NOT the same as for email_set.

None of this is documented. A caller would have to read RFC 8621 §4.7 + RFC 8620 §5.4 separately to understand the error space.

Recommendation: extend the docstring with an "# Errors" section listing the method-level error codes:

  /// # Errors
  ///
  /// Returns `ClientError::MethodError` with one of the following codes
  /// (RFC 8621 §4.7, RFC 8620 §5.4):
  ///
  /// - `fromAccountNotFound` — the `from_account_id` is not accessible.
  /// - `fromAccountNotSupportedByMethod` — the source account does not
  ///    support Email.
  /// - `stateMismatch` — `destroy_from_if_in_state` did not match the
  ///    source-account state.
  /// - `tooManyCopies` / `tooManyEmails` — server-defined caps exceeded.
  ///
  /// Per-entry failures appear in `SetResponse::not_created` as
  /// `SetError` values; per-entry codes include `anchorNotFound` (for
  /// `#`-prefixed result references), `invalidProperties`, `notFound`,
  /// `overQuota`.

Same docstring pattern should apply to email_import (whose error space includes alreadyExists with an existingId extra field — partially documented already) and any other cross-method or unusual-error-space methods.

Priority P3 — pure docs, but a real contract gap for callers writing production error-handling.

Mark says NO HUMAN — clear recommendation.
